//= Expression Evaluation Rules in Functions and Contexts

/'
This document formalizes the rules for when expressions print vs return
in different contexts within the Enzo language interpreter.

KEY PRINCIPLES:
1. Expression statements (like Text atoms, variable invocations, arithmetic)
   normally print their values immediately when encountered
2. Assignment statements (using : <: :>) never print - they only bind values
3. Single-line function atoms have special implicit return behavior for their last statement
4. Function atoms that don't execute (falsy conditions) don't run any contents
'/

//= Rule 1: Top-level expressions print immediately

"this should print";                    // ✓ prints: Text expression at top level
$x: 5;                                 // ✗ doesn't print: assignment statement
$x;                                    // ✓ prints: variable invocation expression
$x + 3;                               // ✓ prints: arithmetic expression

//= Rule 2: Assignment statements never print (regardless of context)

$variable-name: "this shouldn't print";           // ✗ assignment - never prints
$number-var: 42;                                  // ✗ assignment - never prints
"some value" :> $rebind-var;                      // ✗ rebinding - never prints
$list-var <: [1, 2, 3];                          // ✗ rebinding - never prints

//= Rule 3: Multiline function atoms - expressions print, assignments don't

(
    "this should print";                          // ✓ prints: Text expression
    $variable-name: "this shouldn't print";       // ✗ doesn't print: assignment
    $x + $y;                                     // ✓ prints: arithmetic expression
    $another-var: "also no print";              // ✗ doesn't print: assignment
);

//= Rule 4: If statements with multiline function atoms - same rules as Rule 3

If True, (
    "this should print";                          // ✓ prints: Text expression
    $variable-name: "this shouldn't print";       // ✗ doesn't print: assignment
    $result: $x + $y;                            // ✗ doesn't print: assignment
    $result;                                     // ✓ prints: variable invocation
);

//= Rule 5: Function atoms that don't execute - nothing runs

If 0 is 1, (
    "this shouldn't print";                       // ✗ doesn't print: function atom never executes
    $variable-name: "this shouldn't print";       // ✗ doesn't print: function atom never executes
    $x + $y;                                     // ✗ doesn't print: function atom never executes
);

//= Rule 6: Single-line function atoms - implicit return for last statement

If True, ("this should print");                   // ✓ prints via implicit return: last (only) statement

//= Rule 7: Single-line function atoms with multiple statements

If True, ("this should print"; "This should also print");
/'
Breakdown:
- "this should print" - ✓ prints immediately (not last statement, so normal expression printing)
- "This should also print" - ✓ prints via implicit return (last statement of single-line function)
'/

//= Rule 8: Complex single-line function atoms

If True, ($temp: "inner"; "this should print");
/'
Breakdown:
- $temp: "inner" - ✗ doesn't print (assignment statement)
- "this should print" - ✓ prints via implicit return (last statement of single-line function)
'/

If True, ($temp: "inner"; $other: "also inner"; $temp);
/'
Breakdown:
- $temp: "inner" - ✗ doesn't print (assignment statement)
- $other: "also inner" - ✗ doesn't print (assignment statement)
- $temp - ✓ prints via implicit return (last statement returns "inner")
'/

//= Rule 9: Return statements in multiline functions override implicit behavior

(
    "this prints immediately";                    // ✓ prints: expression in multiline
    $temp: "stored value";                       // ✗ doesn't print: assignment
    return($temp);                               // ✓ explicit return overrides - returns "stored value"
);

//= SUMMARY OF RULES:

/'
EXPRESSION PRINTING LOGIC:
1. Assignment statements (:, <:, :>) NEVER print in any context
2. Expression statements (Text, numbers, variable invocations, arithmetic) normally print immediately
3. EXCEPTION: Last statement of single-line function atom gets implicitly returned instead of printing
4. Function atoms that don't execute (falsy If conditions) run no statements at all
5. Explicit return() statements override all implicit behavior

CONTEXTS:
- Top-level: expressions print, assignments don't
- Multiline function atoms (...): expressions print, assignments don't
- Single-line function atoms (...): all statements except last print normally, last statement returns implicitly
- If statements: execute their function atoms which follow the above rules
- Non-executing contexts (falsy If): nothing runs at all
'/